    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. just to be safe, what if i have other stuff like that too? like a high end monitor or a new desk? is there a specific dollar amount where it stops being an \"expense\" and becomes something else i have to depreciate over a few years? i dont want to mess up my filing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a very smart question, and it touches on the difference between immediate expensing and depreciation. For smaller items, like a monitor or a desk, if they fall under a certain threshold—often referred to as the \"de minimis\" safe harbor—you can usually expense them right away in the year you buy them. For very expensive items, like a massive, custom-built desk, you might need to depreciate them over several years, which is like spreading the cost out over the life of the asset.\n\nIt's a bit complex, but generally, if it's a small, consumable tool, you expense it; if it's a major piece of equipment that lasts a long time, you depreciate it. You should check the current IRS guidelines for the exact dollar limit, but for most freelancers, items under a few thousand dollars can often be expensed immediately.\n\nIf you're dealing with a lot of expensive equipment, it's wise to consult with a tax professional who can look at your specific situation. They can help you navigate the depreciation schedules, which is much more precise than trying to guess the right amount yourself. Don't let the paperwork overwhelm you; a quick chat with an",
      "source": "target",
